    slug: "texas-grand-prairie-attorney-tax-lien-ponzi-scheme",
    description: `Federal prosecutors in the Northern District of Texas arrested Grand Prairie attorney David Thomas Gilchrist, 70, on August 31, 2026, charging him with wire fraud, aggravated identity theft, and witness tampering after accusing him of running a years-long Ponzi scheme built around fake property-tax-lien investments and then obstructing the Securities and Exchange Commission's investigation into it. Gilchrist, who had been licensed to practice law in Texas since 1983, pitched roughly twenty victims on promissory notes and partnerships pitched as a vehicle for purchasing property tax liens across Texas counties, taking in approximately $1.45 million between April 2023 and January 2026 — but bank records showed he never used the money to buy tax liens at all, instead commingling it with other funds to cover personal expenses and to repay earlier investors, the classic structure of a Ponzi scheme; only about $789,000 was ever returned. When the SEC opened an inquiry and requested documentation, prosecutors allege Gilchrist submitted a set of purported quitclaim deeds that turned out to be forgeries, and falsely built the scheme's paper trail around an innocent woman who had no real role in it. Days before that woman was set to testify, prosecutors say Gilchrist showed up unannounced at her home, warned her husband that the FBI was looking for him, and urged the couple to "take a vacation" for a few years, suggesting Mexico — an act charged as witness tampering. The case is a reminder that a promissory note pitched by a licensed attorney carries no special guarantee of legitimacy, and that a real property-tax-lien investment can and should be verified independently through the county tax office where the lien is supposedly being purchased, rather than taken on the say-so of the person selling it. Anyone who believes they were solicited by Gilchrist or a similar scheme is urged to contact the FBI or file a complaint with the SEC.`,
